1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:14,416
Three companies each have one job to fill, and we will call the jobs Approximation, Basis and Control.

2
00:00:14,416 --> 00:00:21,192
Three candidates, Anita, Bridget and Christine, are each looking for exactly one job.

3
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:26,726
Every job has ranked the candidates from the one it wants most to the one it wants least.

4
00:00:26,726 --> 00:00:33,656
Approximation would hire Anita first, then Bridget, and Christine comes last on its list.

5
00:00:33,816 --> 00:00:39,890
Basis sees it differently, because it puts Bridget first, then Anita, then Christine.

6
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:46,808
Control has the same list as Approximation, so Anita is at the top for two of the three jobs.

7
00:00:46,983 --> 00:00:52,775
The candidates have opinions too, and each of them has ranked the three jobs in the same way.

8
00:00:52,775 --> 00:00:57,382
Anita likes Basis best, then Approximation, then Control,

9
00:00:57,382 --> 00:01:04,091
while Bridget and Christine both put Approximation first, then Basis, then Control.

10
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:11,048
Our task is a matching, which means every job gets one candidate and nobody is used twice.

11
00:01:11,048 --> 00:01:18,854
Here is one, where Approximation takes Bridget, Basis takes Christine, and Control takes Anita.

12
00:01:19,016 --> 00:01:24,228
But look at Approximation, which got Bridget although Anita is higher on its list.

13
00:01:24,228 --> 00:01:30,326
And Anita got Control, the last job on her list, although she ranks Approximation higher.

14
00:01:30,326 --> 00:01:35,900
So these two would both rather have each other, and a matching like that will not hold.

15
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:43,871
So let us try the obvious thing and let every job ask for the candidate at the top of its list.

16
00:01:43,871 --> 00:01:50,597
Approximation asks Anita, Basis asks Bridget, and Control asks Anita as well.

17
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:56,322
Now Anita holds two offers and Christine holds none, so the obvious thing has failed.

18
00:01:56,322 --> 00:02:03,716
Anita can only take one job, so let her choose, and her own list puts Approximation above Control.

19
00:02:03,883 --> 00:02:08,749
So Anita keeps the offer from Approximation and says no to Control.

20
00:02:08,749 --> 00:02:14,371
Bridget has a single offer, from Basis, so she simply keeps that one.

21
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:17,202
Notice that Anita has not said yes.

22
00:02:17,202 --> 00:02:23,584
Basis is at the top of her list and might still ask her on a later day, so her answer is only a maybe.

23
00:02:23,584 --> 00:02:28,040
We say that she has the offer from Approximation in hand.

24
00:02:28,216 --> 00:02:32,836
Control has been refused, so there is no point in asking Anita again.

25
00:02:32,836 --> 00:02:38,468
It crosses her off its list, and the best candidate it has left is Bridget.

26
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:43,816
What we just watched was one full day, so let us give its parts their names.

27
00:02:43,816 --> 00:02:49,072
In the morning every job made an offer to the best candidate still on its list.

28
00:02:49,072 --> 00:02:54,740
In the afternoon every candidate kept her best offer in hand and refused the rest.

29
00:02:54,740 --> 00:03:00,474
In the evening every refused job crossed off the candidate who said no.

30
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:07,480
On the second morning the rule is the same, so every job asks the best candidate who has not refused it.

31
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:14,348
Approximation and Basis were never refused, so they simply repeat their offers to Anita and Bridget.

32
00:03:14,348 --> 00:03:17,810
Control asks Bridget for the first time.

33
00:03:17,983 --> 00:03:24,197
So this afternoon it is Bridget who holds two offers, one from Basis and one from Control.

34
00:03:24,197 --> 00:03:31,235
Her list puts Basis above Control, so she keeps Basis in hand and refuses Control.

35
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:34,980
In the evening Control crosses Bridget off as well.

36
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:40,166
Only one name is left on its list now, and that name is Christine.

37
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:47,604
On the third morning Approximation asks Anita again, Basis asks Bridget again, and Control asks Christine.

38
00:03:47,604 --> 00:03:55,210
In the afternoon every candidate holds exactly one offer, so for the first time nobody is refused.

39
00:03:55,383 --> 00:03:57,981
Think about what a fourth day would look like.

40
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:04,861
Nobody was refused, so no list changed in the evening, and every job would ask the same candidate again.

41
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:09,741
Each candidate would get the same single offer, so nothing could ever change.

42
00:04:09,741 --> 00:04:15,439
This is where we stop, and each candidate accepts the offer she has in hand.

43
00:04:16,616 --> 00:04:23,538
So the result is that Approximation hires Anita, Basis hires Bridget, and Control hires Christine.

44
00:04:23,538 --> 00:04:29,516
The pair that spoiled our first matching, Approximation and Anita, has ended up together.

45
00:04:29,516 --> 00:04:33,522
This procedure is called the propose and reject algorithm,

46
00:04:33,522 --> 00:04:37,044
and it is also known as the Gale Shapley algorithm.

47
00:04:37,216 --> 00:04:42,606
Look at Control and Christine, who each ended up with the last name on their own list.

48
00:04:42,606 --> 00:04:47,298
And we watched only one example, which happened to stop after three days.

49
00:04:47,298 --> 00:04:49,260
Does this procedure always stop,

50
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:53,982
and is its result always free of pairs who would both rather have each other?

51
00:04:53,982 --> 00:04:57,516
Those are the questions for the next episodes.

